B) One-way, weak collision resistance, and strong collision resistance. 

A cryptographic hash function is designed to ensure that it's computationally infeasible to generate the same hash value (collision) from different input messages (one-way property). Weak collision resistance ensures that an attacker cannot find two distinct messages with the same hash under a practical amount of time and resources. Strong collision resistance further asserts that it is impossible for an attacker to devise two different inputs producing the same output hash, even with unbounded computational power. 

The other options are incorrect as: 
A) Block size and cipher mode pertain to encryption algorithms, not hash functions;
C) Compression and efficiency might be desirable properties but aren't defining characteristics of cryptographic hash functions;
D) Randomness and large output size can contribute to the security of a hash function but do not capture its essential property as outlined above.

